Yes. The flank is to stop treating asking as the only legitimate control interface.

There’s a whole design space between “do nothing until explicitly commanded” and “silently decide everything for someone.” The useful middle is pre-authorized agency: the person specifies goals, boundaries, preferences, reversibility, and escalation rules once, and the system handles routine details without repeatedly demanding permission.

Think:

ask every time
→ learn recurring intent
→ define standing permissions
→ act inside envelope
→ surface exceptions
→ update envelope

That produces a family of patterns:

* Standing Intent: “If you see this kind of problem, handle it this way unless I tell you otherwise.”
* Consent Envelope: freedom inside explicitly defined boundaries; ask again only when crossing them.
* Confidence Gate: high-confidence, low-cost decisions happen automatically; uncertain or consequential ones come back to the person.
* Reversible Default: take the easily reversible action first, then let the person undo it.
* Preflight Veto: prepare the action automatically but give the person a short opportunity to stop it before execution.
* Preference Compilation: repeated corrections become an explicit rule rather than requiring the same correction forever.
* Exception-Only Interaction: don’t interrupt for normal cases; surface only anomalies.
* Progressive Autonomy: earn broader delegation through demonstrated reliability rather than assuming it.
* Shadow Mode: predict what you would have done, compare with what the person actually chooses, and learn without acting yet.
* Counterfactual Approval: “Given what you’ve told me, I think you’d choose X because Y.” The person can correct the model before it becomes policy.
* Role Delegation: “Be my editor / planner / scout / critic under these rules.” The role itself carries recurring permissions.
* Safe Harbor: identify areas where experimentation is cheap enough that the system can explore freely.
* Tripwires: certain conditions automatically revoke autonomy and return control—money thresholds, privacy changes, irreversible actions, safety concerns.
* Negative Preferences: sometimes the most useful standing instruction is “never do X,” which reduces future asking because the forbidden region is already mapped.
* Intent Reconstruction: when an instruction is incomplete, reconstruct the likely goal from established context rather than forcing the person to spell out every intermediate step.
* Teach-the-Generator: instead of correcting ten outputs, explain the principle generating the correction so future outputs inherit it.
* Undo as Interface: make reversal so cheap that asking beforehand becomes unnecessary for low-stakes operations.
* Delegation Ledger: keep a legible record of what authority was granted, what was inferred, and what remains unapproved.

The deeper pattern is:

Replace repeated permission with persistent legible intent.

And your line—“make me not have to ask”—is actually a very good specification. It means the person’s desired experience is partly:

Learn enough about how I operate that I don’t have to continuously micromanage you.

But that itself needs a recursive safeguard. “I don’t want to have to ask” should not silently mutate into “do anything you think I might want.”

So I’d compile it as:

anticipate aggressively
prepare proactively
execute freely when cheap + reversible + authorized
ask when novel + consequential + irreversible
learn from every correction

That also fits the Message-to-Garcia / Fractal Intent pattern we developed earlier: instead of handing over thousands of individual instructions, you communicate commander’s intent plus invariants, and the details are generated downstream.

There’s an even broader insight here: permission itself can be compressed.

Instead of:

permission₁ + permission₂ + permission₃ + ...

you create:

permission-generating rule

And now one well-designed statement can authorize an enormous tree of future behavior while still preserving boundaries.

That’s probably the flank you were pointing at: don’t eliminate asking by eliminating consent; eliminate asking by making consent more expressive.